// GAP (2026-08-03): the spawn-ARG gate is CARRIER-BLIND.
//
// `spawn worker(h)` with a bare Handle(T) is correctly REJECTED
//   ("cannot pass Handle to spawn — pool.get() is not thread-safe").
// Wrapping the SAME handle in a by-value struct slips the gate entirely:
// the check tests the bare argument kind and never descends into a struct
// that CARRIES a handle/pointer.
//
// This is the documented "wrapper hides the inner kind" family (CLAUDE.md),
// specifically the by-value-struct-carrying-a-pointer shape (the §C7/§D1
// form from the 2026-08-01 sweep). tools/audit_carrier_dispatch.sh exists
// for exactly this class but does not cover the spawn-arg sink.
//
// VERIFIED the worker really derefs across the thread boundary — emitted C:
//   worker(): _zer_pool_get(p.slots, p.gen, p.used, ..., b.h, 4)->v
//   main():   p.free(h)   racing it
// so this is a genuine cross-thread use-after-free, not a vacuous probe.
//
// Backstop status: for Handle the runtime generation check still fires
// (subject to the §2 wraparound in docs/Linear-UAF.md). For a raw heap *T
// the same laundering works and only the Level 2/3/5 malloc machinery is
// left. Neither is a compile-time guarantee.
//
// FIX: descend into by-value struct/union args at the spawn-arg gate using
// an existing carrier predicate (type_carries_data_pointer /
// type_can_carry_pointer) rather than testing the bare kind. Then extend
// tools/audit_carrier_dispatch.sh to cover the spawn-arg sink so the next
// carrier shape cannot regress it silently.
//
// gap-open: this file must COMPILE. When it starts being rejected, verify
// the reason is the spawn-arg carrier rule, then promote to zer_fail/.
struct T{u32 v;}
struct Box{ Handle(T) h; }
Pool(T,4) p;

void worker(Box b){ u32 x = b.h.v; }

u32 main(){
    Handle(T) h = p.alloc() orelse { return 1; };
    Box b;
    b.h = h;
    spawn worker(b);
    p.free(h);
    return 0;
}
